# tests/atomic_groups/atomic_caps.rxt -- module `atomic-groups` ([M6.4.2]):
# CAPTURES inside and around a cut.
#
# THIS FILE IS CUT-INV, THE LOWERING'S LOAD-BEARING INVARIANT, AS BEHAVIOUR.
# `RX_CUT` discards resume FRAMES and deliberately does NOT rewind the TRAIL --
# the frames are dead, the capture writes they would have rewound are not,
# because a failure OUTSIDE the group still has to restore the group's groups to
# their pre-group values. The invariant has two halves and only ONE of them
# discriminates:
#
#   RETENTION (section 1). `(?>(a)|ab)` on "ab" is (0,1) with group 1 = (0,1):
#     a capture written inside the body on the path the cut COMMITS TO. A cut
#     that wrongly rewound the trail erases it.
#   UNDO (section 2). `((?>(a)|ab))c|(abc)` on "abc" is (0,3) with groups 1 and
#     2 UNSET and group 3 = (0,3). This half does NOT discriminate the
#     invariant, and the reason is worth knowing rather than embarrassing: a cut
#     that rewinds the trail gets undo trivially right, because it did the undo
#     early. The design's own prototype named this row as the one the question
#     turns on and was wrong; only retention can catch it.
#
# Section 3 is the possessive spellings' own capture behaviour -- `(a)*+` on
# "aaa" reports group 1 = (2,3), the LAST iteration, exactly as the uncut
# spelling does, because possessification changes the machinery and not what the
# quantifier matches.
#
# EVERY EXPECTATION IN THIS FILE WAS PRODUCED BY libpcre2 10.46, not written by
# hand: a generator drove every (pattern, subject, startpos) cell through the
# ctypes binding at docs/design/eng_brep_measurements/probes/pcre2_ctypes.py and
# emitted the answer. python3 `re` was driven over the SAME cells in the same
# pass, and a block carries `# pcre2-only` exactly where python disagreed or
# could not compile the pattern -- detected, never assumed. 13 of 729 cells
# diverge across this directory, and they are the four families Appendix B.3 of
# the design names: `\K`/`\G` (python cannot express them at all), the BRACE
# possessive over a two-exit body (python cuts PER ITERATION, PCRE2 at the GROUP
# EXIT), U9 (libpcre2's own recorded oddity), and scoped `(?i)` inside a group
# (python rejects the pattern).
#
# `ms`/`ns` CELLS SWEEP EVERY STARTPOS IN [0, n], not a chosen few. The cut
# makes "the leftmost candidate is not the leftmost match" an ORDINARY
# occurrence rather than an edge case -- `(?>a|ab)c` on "abc" is NO MATCH while
# its uncut twin matches (0,3) -- so which start the search reaches, and what it
# answers there, is the axis this construct moves.

# ====================================================================
# RETENTION -- the half that DISCRIMINATES the trail invariant
# ====================================================================

pattern (?>(a)|ab)
features atomic-groups
m "ab" 0 1
g 1 0 1
ns 1 "ab"
ns 2 "ab"
m "a" 0 1
g 1 0 1
ns 1 "a"

pattern (?>(a)x|ab)
features atomic-groups
m "ax" 0 2
g 1 0 1
ns 1 "ax"
ns 2 "ax"
m "axb" 0 2
g 1 0 1
ns 1 "axb"
ns 2 "axb"
ns 3 "axb"
m "ab" 0 2
g 1 -1 -1
ns 1 "ab"
ns 2 "ab"

# ====================================================================
# A CAPTURE ABANDONED INSIDE THE BODY, before the cut
# ====================================================================

pattern (?>(a)x|ab)y
features atomic-groups
m "aby" 0 3
g 1 -1 -1
ns 1 "aby"
ns 2 "aby"
ns 3 "aby"
m "axy" 0 3
g 1 0 1
ns 1 "axy"
ns 2 "axy"
ns 3 "axy"

# ====================================================================
# UNDO on an outer failure -- the half no cut sabotage can break
# ====================================================================

pattern ((?>(a)|ab))c|(abc)
features atomic-groups
m "abc" 0 3
g 1 -1 -1
g 2 -1 -1
g 3 0 3
ns 1 "abc"
ns 2 "abc"
ns 3 "abc"
m "ac" 0 2
g 1 0 1
g 2 0 1
g 3 -1 -1
ns 1 "ac"
ns 2 "ac"

# ====================================================================
# THE POSSESSIVE SPELLINGS' own capture behaviour
# ====================================================================

pattern (a)*+
features atomic-groups
m "aaa" 0 3
g 1 2 3
ms 1 "aaa" 1 3
g 1 2 3
ms 2 "aaa" 2 3
g 1 2 3
ms 3 "aaa" 3 3
g 1 -1 -1
m "" 0 0
g 1 -1 -1

pattern (a|b)*+c
features atomic-groups
m "abc" 0 3
g 1 1 2
ms 1 "abc" 1 3
g 1 1 2
ms 2 "abc" 2 3
g 1 -1 -1
ns 3 "abc"
m "c" 0 1
g 1 -1 -1
ns 1 "c"

pattern (?>(a))*b
features atomic-groups
m "aab" 0 3
g 1 1 2
ms 1 "aab" 1 3
g 1 1 2
ms 2 "aab" 2 3
g 1 -1 -1
ns 3 "aab"
m "b" 0 1
g 1 -1 -1
ns 1 "b"
